"Oxford CEBM 2009 levels","structure":"SEPIO assertion / evidence line / evidence item"},"search_url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=gamma-Curcumene%5BAll%20Fields%5D"},"summary":null,"evidence":"Measured in 4 of 6 MONDAYS hemp SKUs at 0.05–0.3% of total volatiles (0.01–0.06mg per chew).","trials":{"total":0,"matched_loosely":0,"studies":[],"source":"ClinicalTrials.gov","search_url":"https://clinicaltrials.gov/search?intr=gamma-Curcumene"},"is_terpenoid":true,"consumer_summary":{"model":"gemini-3.1-pro-preview","generated":"2026-09-11","what_it_is":"Gamma-curcumene is a sesquiterpene, a type of aromatic molecule that plants produce to interact with their environment. As a volatile organic compound, it contributes to the complex, earthy, and herbal scents of various flowers and shrubs. You are most likely to encounter it in the essential oils of aromatic herbs or when brushing past wild daisies and evergreens.","found_in":"This molecule is notably found in the essential oils of Helichrysum (often called the curry plant or everlasting flower), as well as in certain Citrus plants and Artemisia species. It is also present in the foliage of the Nootka cypress tree.","why_researchers_care":"Researchers are drawn to gamma-curcumene because it is a key component in the essential oils of plants long valued in traditional herbalism, particularly Helichrysum. They are actively exploring its potential to fight off microbes, soothe inflammation, and protect cells from oxidative stress. The strongest evidence so far comes from human observational studies (Oxford level 2b) looking at the biological properties of the complex plant extracts containing this molecule.","structure_function":[{"claim_id":"antimicrobial","statement":"May support the body's natural defenses against microbial challenges.","evidence_level":"human_observational","basis":"This rests on 4 papers, including human observational and in vitro studies.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":2},{"design":"in_vitro","eco":"ECO:0000181","papers":2}],"paper_count":4},{"claim_id":"antioxidant","statement":"Is being studied for its potential to support cellular health by neutralizing free radicals.","evidence_level":"human_observational","basis":"This is based on 3 papers, including human observational research.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":1}],"paper_count":3},{"claim_id":"anti-inflammatory","statement":"Early work points toward a role in supporting healthy inflammatory responses.","evidence_level":"human_observational","basis":"This stems from 2 papers, including human observational data.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":1}],"paper_count":2},{"claim_id":"insect","statement":"May act as a natural deterrent against insects.","evidence_level":"animal","basis":"This is supported by 2 animal-model studies.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0007120","label":"animal model system study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"5","label":"bench or animal research"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":2}],"paper_count":2}],"hypothesized_benefits":[{"claim_id":"antimicrobial","area":"Antimicrobial activity","hypothesis":"Researchers are exploring whether gamma-curcumene-rich extracts can effectively inhibit bacterial and fungal growth on human skin or in wounds.","evidence_level":"human_observational","paper_count":4,"study_to_test_it":"A randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial applying a standardized topical gamma-curcumene preparation to superficial skin abrasions, measuring microbial load and healing time over 14 days.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":2},{"design":"in_vitro","eco":"ECO:0000181","papers":2}]},{"claim_id":"antioxidant","area":"Cellular protection","hypothesis":"If this bears out, gamma-curcumene could be a valuable ingredient in dermatological products designed to protect the skin from environmental oxidative damage.","evidence_level":"human_observational","paper_count":3,"study_to_test_it":"A randomized controlled trial in healthy adults applying a gamma-curcumene serum versus a vehicle control, measuring skin lipid peroxidation markers and UV-induced erythema over 8 weeks.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":1}]},{"claim_id":"anti-inflammatory","area":"Tissue soothing","hypothesis":"Early observational work points toward a soothing effect, prompting researchers to ask if this molecule can calm localized tissue inflammation.","evidence_level":"human_observational","paper_count":2,"study_to_test_it":"A randomized, vehicle-controlled human trial evaluating topical gamma-curcumene for contact dermatitis, measuring redness, swelling, and inflammatory cytokine levels in skin patches over 7 days.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":1}]},{"claim_id":"insect","area":"Insect repellent","hypothesis":"Researchers are testing whether this volatile molecule can effectively mask human scents or directly repel biting insects.","evidence_level":"animal","paper_count":2,"study_to_test_it":"A human arm-in-cage behavioral assay testing a standardized gamma-curcumene lotion against a DEET control, measuring the complete protection time (CPT) against mosquito landings over 8 hours.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0007120","label":"animal model system study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"5","label":"bench or animal research"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":2}]}],"associated_conditions":[],"cautions":null,"evidence_at_a_glance":"Best evidence so far is human observational work (Oxford 2b) evaluating plant extracts containing the molecule, supported by a handful of animal and laboratory studies."}}
